-- Q2 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A researcher isolates a single cardiac muscle cell and observes that it contains only one centrally located nucleus, unlike a neighboring skeletal muscle fiber that contains many nuclei. Which developmental event most directly accounts for the single nucleus of the cardiac muscle cell?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its nuclei migrate inward and merge into one"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its myoblasts develop without fusing together"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its myoblasts fuse to form a syncytium"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It loses extra nuclei after maturation"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because cardiac muscle cells retain a single nucleus since the myoblasts that form them do not fuse, unlike the fusing myoblasts that make skeletal muscle multinucleate. During development, a myoblast is a muscle-forming stem cell, and in skeletal muscle many myoblasts fuse into a syncytium (myotube) in which the nucleus of each contributing myoblast remains intact, producing a multinucleate fiber. By contrast, the textbook states that cardiac and smooth muscle cells are not multinucleate because the myoblasts that form their cells do not fuse, so each mature cardiac cell keeps the single nucleus of one myoblast. The single nucleus of cardiac muscle is therefore the default outcome of myoblasts that never combine, not the result of any merging or loss of nuclei. (Choice A) Nuclei do not migrate inward and merge into one; cardiac muscle simply never accumulates multiple nuclei because its myoblasts do not fuse, and nuclear merging is not a described mechanism. (Choice B) This is correct: cardiac (and smooth) muscle cells are uninucleate precisely because their myoblasts do not fuse, leaving each cell with the single nucleus of its founding myoblast. (Choice C) Fusion of myoblasts into a syncytium is exactly what produces the multinucleate skeletal muscle fiber, the opposite of what happens in cardiac muscle, so it would yield many nuclei rather than one. (Choice D) Cardiac cells do not start multinucleate and then shed nuclei after maturation; they begin and remain uninucleate because no fusion occurs in the first place. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that cardiac and smooth muscle remain uninucleate because their myoblasts do not fuse, whereas skeletal muscle is multinucleate due to myoblast fusion.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
