-- Q3 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A histologist examines a tissue sample from the wall of the small intestine and notes that the spindle-shaped cells contain a single nucleus and show a uniform, nonstriated appearance under the light microscope. Which feature of these cells most directly accounts for the absence of visible striations?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cells contain no actin or myosin filaments"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cells lack troponin to bind \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The actin and myosin are not arranged in sarcomeres"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The dense bodies have replaced all thick filaments"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because smooth muscle appears nonstriated since its actin and myosin contractile proteins are not arranged in the regular, banded sarcomere pattern seen in skeletal and cardiac muscle. Striations are the visible stripes produced when actin and myosin are organized in a highly regular, repeating register that a light microscope can resolve, as occurs in the sarcomeres of skeletal and cardiac fibers. Because the actin and myosin are not arranged in such a regular fashion in smooth muscle, the cytoplasm of a smooth muscle fiber has a uniform, nonstriated appearance, which gives smooth muscle its name. Smooth muscle fibers do still contain actin and myosin and thick and thin filaments, but these lack the sarcomere register required to produce stripes. (Choice A) Smooth muscle fibers do have actin and myosin contractile proteins and thick and thin filaments; their presence is not what is missing, so the absence of striations cannot be due to a lack of these filaments. (Choice B) Smooth muscle does lack troponin, but this absence explains why cross-bridge formation is regulated by calmodulin rather than the troponin-tropomyosin complex, not why the fiber appears nonstriated. (Choice C) Is correct: the lack of a regular sarcomere arrangement of actin and myosin is precisely why no banded striations are visible. (Choice D) Dense bodies anchor thin filaments and are analogous to the Z-discs of striated muscle, but they do not replace thick filaments, which are still present in smooth muscle. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that striations arise from the regular sarcomere arrangement of actin and myosin and that smooth muscle lacks this organization.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
