-- Q4 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A researcher isolates a single skeletal muscle fiber from the quadriceps and bathes it in a physiological solution. To make this fiber contract, the stimulus must most directly come from which source?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A circulating hormone reaching the fiber"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fiber''s own intrinsic rhythmic activity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mechanical stretch of the fiber membrane"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A somatic motor neuron firing at the neuromuscular junction"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because excitation signals delivered by a somatic motor neuron at the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) are the only way to functionally activate a skeletal muscle fiber to contract. Every skeletal muscle fiber is innervated by a motor neuron at the NMJ, and the textbook states that "Excitation signals from the neuron are the only way to functionally activate the fiber to contract" (lines 17217-17218). Unlike cardiac and smooth muscle, skeletal muscle "completely depends on signaling from the nervous system to work properly," so the "excitation" step in skeletal muscle is always triggered by the somatic motor division of the nervous system (lines 17054, 17255-17256). The voluntary movements skeletal muscle produces therefore require neural drive routed through the NMJ. (Choice A) A circulating hormone can trigger cardiac and smooth muscle, which "can respond to other stimuli, such as hormones and local stimuli," but skeletal muscle has no such hormonal activation pathway. (Choice B) Intrinsic rhythmic activity describes cardiomyocytes, which "contract on their own intrinsic rhythms without any external stimulation"-a property skeletal muscle fibers lack. (Choice C) Mechanical stretch can trigger smooth muscle (the stress-relaxation response), but stretch does not functionally activate a skeletal muscle fiber, which still requires neural excitation. (Choice D) A somatic motor neuron firing at the NMJ is correct because this is the sole functional route to skeletal muscle contraction; without this neural excitation the fiber cannot contract regardless of other conditions in the bath. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that skeletal muscle is voluntary and depends exclusively on somatic motor neuron excitation at the NMJ for activation.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
