-- Q5 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A histologist examines a stained tissue sample and observes short, branched fibers, each with a single central nucleus, joined to neighboring fibers at their ends by distinct transverse bands. The transverse band joining the fibers end-to-end most likely identifies the tissue as which muscle type?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Cardiac muscle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Skeletal muscle"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Single-unit smooth muscle"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Multiunit smooth muscle"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the transverse bands joining fibers end-to-end are intercalated discs, structures found only in cardiac muscle. Cardiac muscle fibers are short, branched, usually contain a single central nucleus, and are connected to one another at their ends by intercalated discs, which contain gap junctions and desmosomes that let the cells contract in a coordinated wave-like pattern so the heart works as a pump (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 18019-18021, 18035-18036). No other muscle type uses intercalated discs to join its cells end-to-end. (Choice A) Cardiac muscle is correct: the combination of short, branched, single-nucleus fibers joined end-to-end by intercalated discs is diagnostic of cardiac tissue, which is found only in the heart. (Choice B) Skeletal muscle is wrong because its fibers are long, unbranched, and multinucleated, and they are not joined to each other by intercalated discs. (Choice C) Single-unit smooth muscle is wrong because, although its fibers are joined by gap junctions so the muscle contracts as a single unit, those junctions are not packaged into intercalated discs and its cells are spindle-shaped, non-striated, and single-nucleated rather than branched. (Choice D) Multiunit smooth muscle is wrong because its cells rarely possess gap junctions, are not electrically coupled, and likewise lack intercalated discs. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that intercalated discs are the end-to-end junctions unique to cardiac muscle.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
