-- Q6 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A surgeon removes a donor heart and immerses it in a chilled buffer before transplant; with all of its nerve connections severed, the isolated heart continues to beat for several minutes. Which property of cardiac muscle most directly accounts for the continued rhythmic contractions?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Conscious voluntary control of contraction"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Autorhythmicity of self-excitable pacemaker cells"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete dependence on nervous system signaling"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Initiation of each beat by the autonomic nervous system"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because specialized cardiac pacemaker cells are self-excitable and fire action potentials on their own at set intervals, so the heart keeps beating even with every nerve cut. The textbook states that the wave of contraction begins with pacemaker cells, a group that is "self-excitable and able to depolarize to threshold and fire action potentials on their own, a feature called autorhythmicity; they do this at set intervals which determine heart rate" (A&P 2e, lines 18063-18065). Because this depolarization originates within the cardiac tissue itself and spreads through gap junctions to neighboring fibers, no external nervous input is required to start a heartbeat. (Choice A) Conscious voluntary control is wrong because cardiac muscle is involuntary—it "cannot be consciously controlled" (line 18059)—and voluntary control is a feature of skeletal muscle, not the heart. (Choice B) Autorhythmicity is correct: it is precisely the named property that lets pacemaker cells generate beats intrinsically, which is why a denervated heart still contracts. (Choice C) Complete dependence on nervous signaling is wrong because that describes skeletal muscle, which "completely depends on signaling from the nervous system to work properly" (line 17054); cardiac muscle does not, which is the whole reason it keeps beating after denervation. (Choice D) Initiation by the autonomic nervous system is a reversed relationship: the ANS only modulates the rate, sending "signals... to speed up or slow down the heart rate" (lines 18059-18060), but it does not initiate each beat—the pacemaker cells do that themselves. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that cardiac muscle is involuntary yet contracts on an intrinsic rhythm generated by self-excitable pacemaker cells (autorhythmicity), independent of nervous input.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
