-- Q7 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A researcher treats a sheet of cultured cardiac muscle cells with a drug that selectively blocks the gap junctions within their intercalated discs, while leaving the desmosomes intact. When one cell is then stimulated to threshold, which consequence is most likely to follow?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Adjacent cells pull apart during contraction"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Action potentials no longer reach the cell interior"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Depolarizing current no longer spreads to neighboring cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Calcium is no longer released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because gap junctions in the intercalated discs are the channels that let depolarizing current pass directly from one cardiac muscle cell to the next, so blocking them isolates the stimulated cell electrically. The textbook states that "A gap junction forms channels between adjacent cardiac muscle fibers that allow the depolarizing current produced by cations to flow from one cardiac muscle cell to the next," and that this "electric coupling... allows the quick transmission of action potentials and the coordinated contraction of the entire heart," producing "a functional unit of contraction called a syncytium." Remove the gap junctions and the cations carrying the depolarizing current can no longer flow cell-to-cell, so the wave of synchronized contraction cannot spread even though the desmosomes still hold the cells together. (Choice A) Anchoring the cells so they "do not pull apart during the stress of individual fibers contracting" is the job of the desmosomes, which the drug leaves intact, so mechanical separation is not the expected result; this confuses the electrical role of gap junctions with the mechanical role of the other intercalated disc structure. (Choice B) Carrying the action potential "into the interior of the cell" is the function of the T-tubules within a single fiber, an event independent of the intercellular gap junctions, so a stimulated cell still depolarizes internally. (Choice C) is correct, and note that with the syncytium electrically uncoupled, only the one stimulated cell would contract rather than the tissue acting as a coordinated unit. (Choice D) Release of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is a downstream intracellular step of excitation-contraction coupling triggered within the stimulated cell itself, not something that depends on gap junctions, so the directly stimulated cell still releases its \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and contracts. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the role of intercalated disc gap junctions in electrically coupling cardiomyocytes to predict the outcome of selectively blocking them in an experimental preparation.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
