-- Q9 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'As the urinary bladder fills, mechanical stretch of its wall triggers a wave of contraction that spreads across the visceral smooth muscle even before any autonomic input arrives. This coordinated, stretch-initiated response is most directly explained by which feature of single-unit smooth muscle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Gap junctions electrically couple the fibers so stretch-triggered excitation spreads cell to cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sparse gap junctions confine stretch-triggered excitation to the originally stimulated fiber"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Highly organized neuromuscular junctions relay motor-neuron excitation across the fibers"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Circulating hormones depolarize each fiber independently to produce excitation"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle fibers are joined by gap junctions, so the contraction triggered by stretching spreads from cell to cell and the tissue contracts as one unit. In the bladder wall, the muscle fibers are not constrained by the organization and stretchability limits of sarcomeres, so visceral smooth muscle shows a stress-relaxation response in which the mechanical stress of stretching during filling triggers contraction (the textbook states stretching the muscle can trigger its contraction). Because the fibers are electrically coupled through gap junctions, this myogenic excitation propagates across the tissue without requiring autonomic input, producing the coordinated wave described. (Choice A) Correct: gap junctions joining the fibers are exactly what let single-unit smooth muscle contract as a single unit, so a stretch-initiated action potential spreads cell to cell across the bladder wall. (Choice B) This describes multiunit smooth muscle, which rarely possesses gap junctions and is not electrically coupled, so contraction does not spread from one cell to the next and stretch is not even a stimulus for it; it is the wrong tissue type for the bladder. (Choice C) Smooth muscle is not innervated by the highly organized neuromuscular junctions seen in skeletal muscle, and invoking motor-neuron relay also contradicts the premise that the response precedes autonomic input. (Choice D) Hormones are a real trigger for smooth muscle, but hormonal depolarization of each fiber independently would not produce the gap-junction-mediated, stretch-initiated propagation that defines the single-unit response. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that gap junctions electrically couple single-unit visceral smooth muscle and that its stress-relaxation response makes stretch a myogenic trigger for coordinated contraction.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
