-- Q10 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A researcher blocks all calcium entry across the sarcolemma in isolated cardiac, smooth, and skeletal muscle fibers while leaving each cell''s internal calcium stores intact, then stimulates each fiber. Which of the following fiber types are expected to show the GREATEST loss of contractile force under this condition?

I. Skeletal muscle fibers
II. Cardiac muscle fibers
III. Smooth muscle fibers', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because cardiac and smooth muscle both draw most of their contractile \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the extracellular fluid, so blocking sarcolemmal \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry cripples their contraction, whereas skeletal muscle relies on \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) released from its internal sarcoplasmic reticulum. In cardiac muscle, the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores few calcium ions, so most of the calcium ions must come from outside the cells (line 36532-36533). In smooth muscle, the influx of extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions accounts for most of the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) that triggers contraction, because these fibers have a limited calcium-storing SR but have calcium channels in the sarcolemma (lines 18140-18143). By contrast, in skeletal muscle the action potential triggers the release of calcium ions from storage in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, an internal source unaffected by blocking sarcolemmal entry (lines 17331-17332). Thus statements II and III identify the fibers most dependent on extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). (Choice A) Skeletal muscle alone is the one fiber type that would NOT lose most of its force, because its well-developed SR releases the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) needed for contraction independent of extracellular entry. (Choice B) Smooth muscle is correctly extracellular-\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) dependent, but this choice omits cardiac muscle, which the text states must obtain most of its calcium from outside the cell. (Choice C) Pairing skeletal with smooth muscle is a reversed-relationship error: it swaps in skeletal (an SR-dependent fiber) for cardiac (an extracellular-dependent fiber). (Choice D) Cardiac and smooth muscle both have limited SR calcium stores and rely on extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) influx, so both lose the greatest force when sarcolemmal calcium entry is blocked. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the differing calcium sources of the three muscle types to predict which fibers fail when extracellular calcium entry is blocked.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
