-- Q11 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] medium skill4 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A histology lab labels three unknown muscle specimens using only the cues below.

| Specimen | Striations | Nuclei per fiber | Nucleus position | Cell shape |
|----------|-----------|------------------|------------------|------------|
| X | Present | Many | Peripheral | Long cylindrical |
| Y | Present | Single | Central | Short, branched |
| Z | Absent | Single | Central | Short, spindle |

Specimen Y is removed and the technician must predict where it was sampled and what it does. Based on its histology, Specimen Y was most likely sampled from the tissue that performs which function?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Produces voluntary movement of the skeleton"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Contracts rhythmically to pump blood"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Moves food along the digestive tract"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Generates heat by shivering"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the combination of striations, a single central nucleus, and short branched fibers is the unique histological signature of cardiac muscle, which contracts to pump blood in the heart. Table 4.2 lists cardiac muscle as "Short, branched, striated, single central nucleus," located in the "Heart," with the function "Contracts to pump blood"; the text confirms that "cardiomyocytes are single cells typically with a single centrally located nucleus" forming "long, branching cardiac muscle fibers." Specimen Y''s three cues collectively exclude skeletal muscle (which has MANY peripheral nuclei and long cylindrical fibers) and smooth muscle (which lacks striations and is spindle-shaped), leaving cardiac as the only consistent identity. Reading the function off the matching table row therefore pins Specimen Y to the heart''s pumping role. (Choice A) Voluntary movement of the skeleton is the function of skeletal muscle, whose fibers are "Long cylindrical... striated, many peripherally located nuclei" (Specimen X), not the single-central-nucleus branched fiber of Specimen Y. (Choice B) is correct: short branched striated fibers with one central nucleus match cardiac muscle, and Table 4.2 assigns it the function "Contracts to pump blood." (Choice C) Moving food along the digestive tract is smooth muscle''s job; smooth muscle shows "no evident striation, single nucleus" and a spindle shape (Specimen Z), so its lack of striations contradicts Specimen Y''s striated appearance. (Choice D) Heat generation by shivering is a property of skeletal muscle, described in the text as "an involuntary contraction of skeletal muscles," again pointing to Specimen X rather than the cardiac-type Specimen Y. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the histology table cues for Specimen Y and reason from those features to the tissue''s identity and function rather than recalling a memorized fact.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
