-- Q12 [Muscle Types & Characteristics] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Types & Characteristics', 'A physiologist studies multiunit smooth muscle isolated from the wall of a large blood vessel. When a single cell is stimulated, the resulting contraction stays confined to that cell and does not spread to its neighbors, even though the cells are in direct physical contact. The contraction of this tissue is most likely triggered by which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Mechanical stretching of the tissue wall"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Action potentials conducted through gap junctions"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Autonomic nerve signals or circulating hormones"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Spontaneous firing of pacesetter cells"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because multiunit smooth muscle lacks gap junctions and is therefore driven by autonomic nerves or hormones rather than by stretch or cell-to-cell electrical spread. The textbook states that ''Multiunit smooth muscle cells rarely possess gap junctions, and thus are not electrically coupled. As a result, contraction does not spread from one cell to the next, but is instead confined to the cell that was originally stimulated,'' and that ''Stimuli for multiunit smooth muscles come from autonomic nerves or hormones but not from stretching.'' Because each cell is electrically isolated, an external chemical signal delivered by the autonomic nervous system or by circulating hormones is required to activate the cells. (Choice A) Mechanical stretching triggers contraction in single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle via the stress-relaxation response, but the passage explicitly states stimuli for multiunit smooth muscle come from autonomic nerves or hormones ''but not from stretching,'' making stretch the right mechanism for the wrong muscle type. (Choice B) Action potentials conducted through gap junctions describe single-unit smooth muscle, whose fibers are ''joined by gap junctions so that the muscle contracts as a single unit''; multiunit cells rarely possess gap junctions and are not electrically coupled, which is precisely why the contraction in the scenario fails to spread. (Choice C) is correct because autonomic nerve signals or circulating hormones are the stated stimuli for multiunit smooth muscle, consistent with its electrically isolated cells that cannot propagate signals among themselves. (Choice D) Pacesetter cells spontaneously trigger action potentials in visceral (single-unit) smooth muscle of hollow organs ''except the heart''; multiunit tissue around large blood vessels relies on external autonomic or hormonal input rather than intrinsic pacesetter activity. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the absence of gap junctions in multiunit smooth muscle leaves it dependent on autonomic nerves and hormones rather than stretch or electrical coupling for contraction.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
