-- Q13 [Sarcomere Structure] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A histologist examines a longitudinal electron micrograph of a relaxed skeletal muscle fiber and wants to outline the boundaries of a single sarcomere, the fiber''s functional contractile unit. Which span most accurately marks the limits of one sarcomere?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "From one M-line to the next M-line"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The full length of a single myofibril"}, {"label": "C", "text": "From a Z-line to the central M-line"}, {"label": "D", "text": "From one Z-line to the adjacent Z-line"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the sarcomere is defined as the region from one Z-line to the next Z-line, which is the functional unit of a skeletal muscle fiber. Each sarcomere is bordered by Z-discs (also called Z-lines), to which the actin myofilaments are anchored, and it is approximately \(2\ \mu\text{m}\) in length. The thin filaments project from the Z-discs toward the center of the sarcomere, and many sarcomeres are arranged linearly within a myofibril, so the boundary that defines one contractile unit is the span between two consecutive Z-lines. (Choice A) The M-line is the central spot where the thick filaments are anchored at their bases, so it sits in the middle of the sarcomere rather than at its borders; an M-line-to-M-line span shifts the unit by half a sarcomere instead of marking the true boundaries. (Choice B) A myofibril is composed of many sarcomeres running along its length and runs the entire length of the muscle fiber, so it is far larger than a single sarcomere and is not the functional contractile unit being outlined. (Choice C) The span from a Z-line to the central M-line covers only half of a sarcomere, because the M-line lies at the center of the unit, not at its boundary. (Choice D) The Z-lines (Z-discs) anchor the actin filaments and border the unit, so the region from one Z-line to the next correctly defines the full sarcomere. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the structural definition of the sarcomere as the region between adjacent Z-lines.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
