-- Q14 [Sarcomere Structure] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A histologist examines a single skeletal muscle sarcomere under high magnification and traces each contractile filament back to the structure that anchors it. Which pairing of filament to its anchoring structure does the histologist most likely confirm?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Actin anchored at the M-line; myosin anchored at the Z-disc"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Actin anchored at the Z-disc; myosin anchored at the Z-disc"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Actin anchored at the Z-disc; myosin anchored at the M-line"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Actin anchored at the Z-disc; myosin anchored at the I band"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the thin (actin) filaments are anchored at their ends by the Z-discs, while the thick (myosin) filaments are anchored at their bases at the M-line. Within the sarcomere, the Z-discs border each unit and are the structures "to which the actin myofilaments are anchored," with actin projecting from the Z-discs toward the center of the sarcomere as the thin filament. The thick (myosin) filaments occupy the central region of the sarcomere and are "anchored at their bases at a spot called the M-line." Thus the correct filament-to-anchor mapping is actin at the Z-disc and myosin at the M-line. (Choice A) This reverses the relationship: it places actin at the M-line and myosin at the Z-disc, but the textbook anchors thin (actin) filaments at the Z-discs and thick (myosin) filaments at the M-line, so both pairings are flipped. (Choice B) Actin is correctly placed at the Z-disc, but myosin does not anchor at the Z-disc; thick filaments are anchored at their bases at the M-line in the center of the sarcomere, so naming the Z-disc as the myosin anchor confuses the central anchoring point with the bordering disc. (Choice C) This is correct because it matches both anchoring sites the textbook assigns: actin (thin) at the Z-disc and myosin (thick) at the M-line, the central spot where thick filaments are anchored at their bases. (Choice D) Actin is correctly placed at the Z-disc, but the I band is the lighter region that becomes smaller during contraction and contains thin filaments only; it is not the anchoring structure for thick filaments, which are anchored at the M-line. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the structural organization of the sarcomere and which structure anchors each type of myofilament.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
