-- Q15 [Sarcomere Structure] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'In a resting skeletal muscle fiber, cytosolic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is low and the thin filament holds the myosin heads off. Which feature of the thin filament most directly prevents cross-bridge formation under these conditions?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Troponin bound to \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) at its regulatory site"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Myosin heads sequestered along the actin chains"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Actin shielding the regulatory sites on troponin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Tropomyosin covering the myosin-binding sites on actin"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because at rest tropomyosin winds around the actin filament and physically covers the myosin-binding sites, blocking cross-bridge formation. The textbook states that "Tropomyosin is a protein that winds around the chains of the actin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites to prevent actin from binding to myosin," and that the "troponin-tropomyosin complex prevents the myosin ''heads'' from binding to the active sites on the actin microfilaments." Only when \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) rises and binds troponin does "tropomyosin slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands," exposing the sites; at rest, with \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) low, tropomyosin stays in place and the heads cannot attach. (Choice A) Troponin bound to \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) describes the activating, not the resting, state — \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding to troponin is "the first step in the process of contraction" that moves tropomyosin aside, so a \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-occupied troponin would expose the sites rather than block them. (Choice B) The myosin heads project from the thick filament and are the structures held off; they are not part of the thin filament and are not sequestered along actin, so this reverses which filament does the blocking. (Choice C) This reverses the actual relationship: tropomyosin (with troponin) covers sites on actin, not the other way around — actin does not shield troponin''s regulatory site, which is instead where \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds. (Choice D) Tropomyosin covering the myosin-binding sites on actin is the precise resting-state block the passage describes, the structural reason a low-\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) fiber stays relaxed. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the resting-state role of tropomyosin in covering actin''s myosin-binding sites.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
