-- Q16 [Sarcomere Structure] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A physiologist uses high-magnification microscopy to image a single skeletal muscle sarcomere as it shortens during a contraction. Which structural feature most likely remains constant in width throughout the shortening?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The A band"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The I band"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The H zone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The spacing between adjacent Z lines"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the A band corresponds to the full length of the thick (myosin) filaments, which do not change length during contraction, so the A band stays the same width even as the sarcomere shortens. During the sliding filament mechanism, thin filaments are pulled past the thick filaments toward the center of the sarcomere; the filaments themselves do not shorten, they only slide, so the span occupied by the thick filaments (the A band) is preserved. The textbook states directly that "The A band does not shorten—it remains the same length," while the I band and H zone shrink and the Z lines are drawn closer together. Because the thick filaments anchored at the M line define the boundaries of the A band, only changes in overlap (not in the A band itself) occur as the sarcomere contracts. (Choice A) Correct: the A band marks the full length of the thick filaments, and because those filaments slide rather than shorten, its width is constant; this is the classic diagnostic feature distinguishing the sliding filament model from a filament-shortening model. (Choice B) The I band contains only thin filaments not yet overlapped by thick filaments, and it "becomes smaller" as the thin filaments slide inward, so it does not stay constant. (Choice C) The H zone is the central region of the A band that contains only thick filaments, and it "is shortened during contraction" as thin filaments invade it, so it also narrows. (Choice D) The spacing between adjacent Z lines defines sarcomere length, and "the Z lines move closer together" during contraction, so this distance decreases rather than staying constant. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the A band corresponds to the thick-filament length and that filaments slide without shortening.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
