| A band | 1.6 | 1.6 |

Which region''s measured behavior most directly demonstrates that the thick and thin filaments slide past one another rather than shorten during contraction?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The I band, because it narrows"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The A band, because its width is unchanged"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The H zone, because it narrows"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The Z discs, because they move apart"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the A band corresponds to the full length of the thick filaments, so its constant width during contraction shows the thick filaments themselves do not shorten even as the sarcomere does. The sliding filament model holds that the sarcomere shortens because the thin filaments are pulled past the stationary-length thick filaments toward the center of the sarcomere, not because any filament contracts; the textbook states that thick and thin filaments "do not shorten. Instead, they slide by one another, causing the sarcomere to shorten while the filaments remain the same length." Because the A band spans only the thick filaments, an unchanged A band width (1.6 \(\mu\text{m}\) at rest and at peak contraction) is the direct structural readout that filament length is conserved. The I band and H zone do narrow during contraction, but they shrink precisely because thin filaments slide deeper into the overlap region, so their narrowing alone is also consistent with hypothetical filament shortening and does not by itself rule it out. (Choice A) The I band narrows, but the I band contains only thin filaments and its narrowing reflects increased overlap, which would occur whether filaments slid or shortened, so it does not isolate the sliding mechanism. (Choice B) is correct: the A band stays the same width because it equals the thick-filament length, and a constant A band can only result from filaments that keep their length while sliding. (Choice C) The H zone narrows, but it shrinks because thin filaments invade the previously thin-filament-free central region; like the I band, its narrowing does not distinguish sliding from shortening. (Choice D) Z discs do move closer together (not apart) during contraction, which measures that the sarcomere shortens but says nothing about whether the filaments themselves keep their length. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated band-width measurements to infer which observation uniquely supports the sliding filament mechanism.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
