Based on these measurements, which region most likely corresponds to the A band?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(R_1\\), because its width shrinks as the filaments slide"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(R_3\\), because it is the central thick-filament-only zone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(R_2\\), because its width is unchanged between the two states"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(R_3\\), because thin filaments occupy it exclusively"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the A band is the only sarcomere region whose width stays constant during contraction, and \(R_2\) is the region measured at 1.6 \(\mu m\) in both the relaxed and contracted states. According to the sliding filament model, when a sarcomere contracts the Z lines move closer together and the I band becomes smaller, but "The A band stays the same width" because the thick filaments that define it do not shorten and the thin filaments simply slide deeper into it. Therefore the diagnostic signature of the A band in measurement data is invariance: the band that reads the same width before and after contraction is the A band, which is \(R_2\) here. The regions that shrink (\(R_1\) and \(R_3\)) are the I band and the H zone, both of which narrow as the thin filaments are pulled toward the center of the sarcomere. (Choice A) \(R_1\) shrinks from 1.0 to 0.4 \(\mu m\), which is the behavior of the I band, the region that contains only thin filaments and also shortens during contraction; identifying it as the A band reverses the constant-versus-shrinking distinction that the question tests. (Choice B) \(R_3\) is the smallest region and narrows to near zero, matching the H zone, the central region of the A band that contains only thick filaments and is shortened during contraction; the H zone is part of the A band but is not the A band itself, and crucially it does not stay constant. (Choice C) \(R_2\) holds at 1.6 \(\mu m\) across both states, the unique fingerprint of the A band, whose length is set by the non-shortening thick filaments; the thin filaments slide farther into it without changing its width. (Choice D) \(R_3\) is the H zone, which contains only thick filaments, not thin filaments, so the stated rationale reverses the actual filament composition; the H zone also shrinks rather than staying constant, so it cannot be the A band. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the relaxed-versus-contracted width measurements and recognize that constancy, not magnitude, identifies the A band.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
