WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A skeletal muscle fiber is stimulated and its sarcomeres begin to shorten as the thin filaments are pulled inward toward the M line. As the degree of overlap between thick and thin filaments increases during this contraction, the central H zone of each sarcomere most directly does which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Stays the same width as the A band lengthens"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Widens as thin filaments fill the central region"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Disappears as the I band correspondingly widens"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Narrows as thin filaments encroach on the thick-filament-only zone"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the H zone is the central part of the A band that holds only thick filaments, so when contraction pulls the thin filaments inward and increases overlap, those thin filaments intrude on that central region and the H zone narrows. The textbook defines the H zone as the "central region of the A band that only contains thick filaments" (Biology 2e, lines 51343-51345), and it states that "The H zone ... contains only thick filaments and is shortened during contraction" while "The zone of overlap ... increases as the thin filaments move inward" (Biology 2e, lines 51387, 51391-51392). Because the thick-filament-only region is precisely what the advancing thin filaments invade, an increase in overlap is geometrically the same event as a loss of H-zone width. (Choice A) The A band does not lengthen during contraction; the textbook states "The A band does not shorten—it remains the same length" (Biology 2e, line 51388), and the H zone shortens rather than holding constant, so both halves of this option misstate the mechanism. (Choice B) Widening reverses the actual relationship: thin filaments moving inward increase overlap, which consumes the thick-filament-only H zone rather than expanding it, so the H zone shortens during contraction (Biology 2e, line 51387). (Choice C) The H zone can shrink toward disappearance, but the I band does not widen—"The I band contains only thin filaments and also shortens" during contraction (Biology 2e, line 51388)—so the paired claim is incorrect. (Choice D) Correct: the inward sliding of thin filaments raises the overlap and intrudes on the central thick-filament-only band, narrowing the H zone, consistent with the H zone being "shortened during contraction" as "the zone of overlap ... increases" (Biology 2e, lines 51387, 51391-51392). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the structural definition of the H zone and connect it to how increasing filament overlap changes that region during contraction.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
