-- Q22 [Sarcomere Structure] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A researcher treats an isolated skeletal muscle fiber with an agent that selectively seals off the T-tubules from the surface sarcolemma, so depolarization can no longer travel into the fiber''s interior. Which of the following effects on excitation-contraction coupling are most likely to result?

I. The action potential fails to reach the membrane of the sarcoplasmic reticulum deep within the fiber.
II. \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is no longer synthesized within the sarcoplasmic reticulum.
III. \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the sarcoplasm is impaired.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "II and III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "I and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma that carry the action potential into the fiber''s interior to reach the SR, so sealing them blocks both the inward conduction of the action potential (I) and the triggered \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release from the SR (III), while the SR is a store of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), not a site of its synthesis (II). The textbook states that "for the action potential to reach the membrane of the SR, there are periodic invaginations in the sarcolemma, called T-tubules" and that "the T-tubules carry the action potential into the interior of the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing Ca++ to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm." Removing the T-tubule pathway therefore prevents the depolarization from reaching the SR membrane and prevents the gated SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release that initiates contraction. (Choice A) I only is incomplete because, although the action potential indeed fails to reach the SR membrane, that failure necessarily abolishes the downstream \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release (III), so I cannot be the only consequence. (Choice B) II and III only is wrong because it includes statement II; the SR is a "specialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases, and retrieves Ca++"—it does not synthesize calcium ions, which are simply released from storage. (Choice C) III only is incomplete because it omits I; the impaired \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release happens precisely because the action potential can no longer be conducted inward to the SR membrane, so I must also be true. (Choice D) I and III only correctly pairs the loss of inward action-potential conduction with the resulting failure of SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release, and excludes the false claim that the SR synthesizes \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural role of the T-tubule to predict the coupled downstream consequences of removing it.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
