-- Q24 [Sarcomere Structure] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'A pharmacologist studying vascular smooth muscle uses fluorescent tags to follow how the cell transmits the force generated by sliding thin filaments to the cell membrane. The cells contract by pulling their tapered ends toward the center, yet they lack striations and sarcomeres. Tension from the thin filaments is most directly transmitted to the sarcolemma through which structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Z-discs spanning each sarcomere boundary"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Dense bodies fastened to the sarcolemma"}, {"label": "C", "text": "M-lines at the center of the sarcomere"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The troponin-tropomyosin complex on actin"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because in smooth muscle the thin filaments are anchored by dense bodies, which are fastened to the sarcolemma and serve as the functional analog of the Z-discs found in striated muscle. Smooth muscle fibers lack striations and sarcomeres, so they cannot use Z-discs or M-lines; instead, the thin filaments are anchored to dense bodies invested in the inner membrane of the sarcolemma, and when the thin filaments slide past the thick filaments they pull on these dense bodies. The dense bodies are tethered to the sarcolemma and also attach to intermediate filament networks, so the force generated by filament sliding is transmitted through them to the membrane, pulling the ends of the fiber toward its center. Because a dense body is explicitly described as analogous to the Z-discs of skeletal and cardiac muscle and is fastened to the sarcolemma, it is the structure through which thin-filament tension reaches the membrane. (Choice A) Z-discs do anchor thin filaments, but only in skeletal and cardiac muscle, which possess sarcomeres; smooth muscle has no sarcomeres or Z-discs, so this structure is absent from the cell described and dense bodies replace its anchoring role. (Choice B) Dense bodies are correct because they anchor the thin filaments, are fastened to the sarcolemma at adherens junctions, and transmit sliding-filament force to the membrane and intermediate-filament network, exactly mirroring the Z-disc function in striated muscle. (Choice C) M-lines anchor the bases of the thick filaments at the center of the sarcomere, not the thin filaments, and like Z-discs they exist only in striated muscle, so they neither anchor thin filaments nor occur in smooth muscle. (Choice D) The troponin-tropomyosin complex is a regulatory assembly that controls cross-bridge formation in striated muscle, and smooth muscle does not even contain troponin; it regulates contraction rather than anchoring filaments or transmitting force to the sarcolemma. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that smooth muscle dense bodies are the functional analog of striated-muscle Z-discs and anchor the thin filaments to the sarcolemma.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
