-- Q25 [Sarcomere Structure] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Sarcomere Structure', 'In an arteriole, a vasoconstrictor opens \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels in the sarcolemma of a smooth muscle fiber, and the rising sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) drives cross-bridge formation. Which event(s) most directly account for how cross-bridge formation is switched on in this fiber?

I. \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds calmodulin rather than a subunit of troponin
II. A conformational change in troponin slides tropomyosin off the actin binding sites
III. An activated myosin light-chain kinase phosphorylates the myosin heads', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "II only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "II and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "I, II, and III"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because smooth muscle lacks troponin, so \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) acts through calmodulin (statement I) and the resulting complex activates myosin light-chain kinase to phosphorylate the myosin heads (statement III), while the troponin-based step (statement II) cannot occur. In a smooth muscle fiber, external and SR-derived \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) bind calmodulin, and the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-calmodulin complex then activates myosin (light chain) kinase, which activates the myosin heads by phosphorylating them so they can attach to actin and pull on the thin filaments. Because smooth muscle cells do not contain troponin, cross-bridge formation is not regulated by the troponin-tropomyosin complex but instead by the regulatory protein calmodulin, which makes statement II a regulatory route that physically cannot operate here. (Choice A) Correct: statements I and III together describe the entire smooth-muscle switch — \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-calmodulin binding followed by kinase-driven phosphorylation of the myosin heads — with no troponin step. (Choice B) Wrong: statement II is the skeletal/cardiac mechanism in which \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin to move tropomyosin off actin, but smooth muscle has no troponin, so this step is absent. (Choice C) Wrong: it correctly includes the kinase step (III) but pairs it with the troponin step (II), which does not occur in smooth muscle because troponin is not present. (Choice D) Wrong: it adds statement II to the true smooth-muscle events; the troponin-tropomyosin route is the route smooth muscle specifically bypasses. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) regulates cross-bridge formation in smooth muscle through calmodulin and myosin light-chain kinase rather than through the troponin-tropomyosin complex.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
